# M2 per-lab capability factory

Parent: [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]]. The factory is part of each VM's AI4R, initially a module/worker in its app service. It owns independent jobs, source templates, local candidate records and evolution history. Docker Engine owns local images; BuildKit owns separate build/cache state; neither is the eligibility catalog.

## Spark development profile

[[Designs/Spark Shared Services and Storage]] owns storage/memory policy. Each factory remains independent in its CPU VM, but model/repository acquisition is host-managed. Reserve peak bytes before every build, pull, export or repair. Start with one heavyweight storage operation machine-wide and a 30 GB GC target per lab builder, inside the guest disk ceiling. Weights and datasets are not image content.

Generalist gap-filling attempts retain source/evidence deltas rather than unlimited images, contexts, clones or VM snapshots. Parent journeys cap attempts and cumulative new bytes. Publish selected candidates, then reclaim unleased local replicas through reference-aware retention. Registry blobs and each VM's loaded layers consume separate physical allocations. Model calls use host inference through the scoped relay; a STORAGE_BLOCKED result is scheduling, not a scientific verdict.

## Source-to-image pipeline

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    S[Authorized prepared source] --> A[Author declaration and implementation]
    A --> C[Capture immutable build context]
    C --> B[Host broker runs Buildx and BuildKit]
    B --> L[Explicit load into local Engine]
    L --> I[Record local image ID and platform]
    I --> T[Independent sandbox qualification]
    T --> E[Local admission for future plans]
    I --> X[Candidate image and source export]
    T --> X
    X --> Q[Central rebuild and qualification]
    Q --> R[Tested release and registry pins]
```

The qualification path can inspect a candidate before it is admitted for research use, under a separate sandbox-job permission. The factory may submit checks/evidence, but cannot assign its own admission outcome. Export may preserve failed candidates for diagnosis; publication and central adoption are never inferred from a successful upload.

## Build mechanics

Use one named Buildx builder per lab with the `docker-container` driver initially. The broker provisions the tested BuildKit image/version and applies build concurrency/resource limits. BuildKit is itself an infrastructure container on the VM engine. Its cache/state is separate from Engine images; build results require an output exporter. [Docker container driver](https://docs.docker.com/build/builders/drivers/docker-container/).

Illustrative operator commands inside an already prepared VM, not calls available directly to CC agents:

```bash
docker buildx create --name lab-a-builder --driver docker-container
docker buildx build --builder lab-a-builder \
  --platform linux/arm64 --load \
  --tag ai4r-candidate/analysis:attempt-a1 \
  --metadata-file /srv/ai4r/jobs/build-a1/build-metadata.json \
  /srv/ai4r/jobs/build-a1/context
docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' ai4r-candidate/analysis:attempt-a1
```

The actual builder creation pins the selected BuildKit image and limits from release configuration; commands above omit those deployment values rather than inventing them. The candidate tag is a discovery handle only. Resolve and bind the full local image ID before testing/admission. This single-platform `--load` path avoids requiring a registry during every local iteration. A multi-platform release uses separate native builds and registry manifests; do not claim amd64 images execute natively on ARM. [Buildx output/platform options](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/buildx/build/).

For sharing, push the already-qualified local artifact through a candidate-only registry credential or produce a verified image archive. Record registry manifest/platform/local-ID relationships or archive hashes and inspect after import. Central release rebuilds from the submitted context and checks the exact resulting image, which may have a new digest. Registry transfer preserves image content but does not grant admission. A saved image excludes project volumes/state. [Image archive export](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/image/save/).

## Build context, dependencies and secrets

Factory captures a source snapshot before build; it does not build from a mutable live checkout. Hash the context manifest, Dockerfile, declaration, vendored resources and locks. Stage only required files and enforce an exclusion policy: no `.git` internals, credentials, datasets not declared for packaging, lab database or evidence store. Never send the whole lab directory as context.

Resolve prepared dependency/base-image access in a separate preparation stage. The BuildKit container has its own content/cache view; loading a base into Engine alone is not an offline-build guarantee. Qualify the actual builder's base/content availability. `--network=none` for build steps is not a complete offline policy: base pulls/frontends or remote source instructions can still need access. The broker must control allowed context/frontend/base sources and build environment egress, then validate a truly offline profile separately. Normal online factory builds have explicit preparation/build grants, distinct from invocation grants.

Use pinned base references and locked/vendored dependencies where practical. For the first profile, use operator-owned Dockerfile templates and reject arbitrary privileged build entitlements. Generated Dockerfiles and dependency installers remain executable code; same-VM builds do not provide a hostile-build isolation guarantee. Build credentials use scoped temporary secret mounts rather than image ENV/ARG. Build scripts can still misuse credentials they legitimately receive; minimize their scope. [Build secrets](https://docs.docker.com/build/building/secrets/).

## Qualification, jobs and evolution

Independent qualification checks protocol conformance, real invocation, source/reference fidelity where required, dependencies, declared effects and failure cases. Input-dependent checks stay host-owned. Semantic verifier receives only work declaration and output. A paper-only request may produce a reading resource rather than a CC; missing reference data/model/license conditions block or narrow the output explicitly.

Persist job stages: PREPARED, BUILDING, BUILT, QUALIFYING, then CANDIDATE/ELIGIBLE/REJECTED/BLOCKED as appropriate. Persist broker operation IDs so restart reconciles an existing build rather than starting a duplicate. A build command that times out may still have work in progress; confirm cancellation/ownership before resubmission. Changed source, recipe or requirements produces a new attempt and invalidates dependent evidence. Preserve logs and failed images under retention policy.

Bootstrap factories with the tested AI4R app, templates and seed CCs. New factory output is not required to build that same factory. Factory-stage CCs and a later composite workflow can be introduced after stable boundaries are proved. Full CC containerization does not imply every infrastructure function is a CC.

Lab source/platform changes are native Git patches or captured trees, not necessarily CCs. Factory templates, runner, planner and app evolve as distinct release candidates. The active stack does not hot-rewrite itself mid-job. Operator/broker-controlled activation drains or preserves jobs, builds a new app/factory image, runs fixed incumbent checks and switches future work to a compatible release. Database changes need forward/rollback procedures.

## Central contribution and lab independence

Each VM has its own clone, local images/catalog and resources. Candidate export captures parent commit, committed patch range, uncommitted/untracked source snapshot where present, declaration/recipe, immutable images, checks, limitations and evidence hashes. Do not assume the earlier read-only experiment manager already supports this developer export.

Sibling candidates from two labs retain lineage; central tests both against a common baseline and requalifies any merged result. CC-only releases can be adopted independently when compatible. Runner/factory/planner dependencies must be explicit; platform changes have a separate release path. Central publication does not update running labs. A lab chooses selective import or an explicit rebase, with new plans using the updated catalog while frozen runs retain old pins.

First acceptance: A builds one bounded capability, rejects one bad candidate, admits the good candidate locally, and invokes it in a new plan. B independently imports/reconstructs and qualifies it while retaining its own project. Demonstrate interruption, missing dependency, verified export and separate central adoption. Later show a factory/platform change with migration/rollback to establish improvement beyond wrapper generation.
